const PLUM_RAIN_CITY_NAMES={
  '广州':'Guangzhou','深圳':'Shenzhen','珠海':'Zhuhai','汕头':'Shantou','佛山':'Foshan','韶关':'Shaoguan','河源':'Heyuan','梅州':'Meizhou','惠州':'Huizhou','汕尾':'Shanwei','东莞':'Dongguan','中山':'Zhongshan','江门':'Jiangmen','阳江':'Yangjiang','湛江':'Zhanjiang','茂名':'Maoming','肇庆':'Zhaoqing','清远':'Qingyuan','潮州':'Chaozhou','揭阳':'Jieyang','云浮':'Yunfu','洛杉矶':'Los Angeles'
};

const PLUM_RAIN_VOICES={
  'rv-032':{name:'A-Zhe',role:'Field recordist and music maker',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Sounds & Landscapes',tags:['Field recording','Listening'],title:'Banana Leaves, Roof Tiles, and the Canal',hook:'Sitting in an abandoned wooden boat, A-Zhe recorded three distinct sounds of rain.',body:`To collect the sounds of a Lingnan water village, A-Zhe sat alone in an abandoned wooden boat in Xiaozhou Village during a downpour. Rain landed heavily on banana leaves, rang sharply against grey roof tiles, and became a fine rustle as it entered the canal.
With his eyes closed, different plants and materials became clear through sound. The smell of vegetation, damp air, and a slight chill also stayed with him.
He now protects his equipment before recording and later edits and mixes what he hears. Rain once felt irritating; today he listens for the season and the surface it touches. He wants people to pause before watching the rain—and first hear where it lands.`},
  'rv-003':{name:'Uncle Chen',role:'Sanitation worker',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Sanitation','City maintenance'],title:'While They Applauded, He Only Felt Cold',hook:'When the drain finally cleared, people nearby applauded. He only felt cold.',body:`During a storm, leaves and plastic bags blocked a drain and water rose to his knees. Uncle Chen bent down, half-submerged in muddy water, and pulled rubbish from the grate by hand. When the water finally rushed away, people sheltering nearby applauded. He only remembers feeling cold.
His raincoat could not stop water from soaking his clothes. Even after the storm, wet leaves clung to the asphalt and had to be picked up one by one.
After nearly thirty years in Guangzhou, he is used to its fierce rain. He hopes people understand that a clean street after a storm also carries the labour of sanitation workers who stood in it.`},
  'rv-009':{name:'Xiaolin',role:'Metro station attendant',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Metro','Help between strangers'],title:'An Umbrella and a Cup of Milk Tea',hook:'A personal umbrella came back the next day—with a cup of milk tea.',body:`A sudden rush-hour storm trapped passengers at the metro entrance. A high-school student, close to tears, said she still had to reach an exam. Xiaolin ran to the changing room and lent her a spare umbrella. The next day, the student returned it with a cup of milk tea.
Rainy shifts mean laying anti-slip mats, placing warning signs, mopping water, and guiding crowds away from the entrance. Wet coats and umbrellas keep bringing more water inside.
Storm warnings now make Xiaolin think of flood barriers, sandbags, and passenger flow. Yet she also sees strangers sharing umbrellas and parents keeping dry clothes for children. Rain makes these brief acts of care visible.`},
  'rv-028':{name:'Michael',role:'Piano tuner',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Piano','Sound'],title:'The Piano That Would Not Play',hook:'The keys stayed down, and every note sounded as though it came through a heavy quilt.',body:`After a grand piano was left beside a partly open window through a month of rain, Michael found rust on the strings and moisture in the wooden action. More than a dozen keys would not rise after being pressed.
The sound had changed too. Clear notes had become dull and muffled, and the instrument smelled of damp felt and old wood.
The rainy season is his busiest time for tuning and repair. Rain once sounded like natural music; now it also suggests swollen wood and trapped keys. Humidity has acquired a shape that he can hear.`},
  'rv-034':{name:'Sihui',role:'Full-time parent',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Parenting','Puddles'],title:'Her Daughter’s First Puddle',hook:'Her daughter tested the water with one foot, froze, then laughed and jumped straight in.',body:`When her daughter first wore rain boots, Sihui encouraged her to cross a puddle. The child stepped in carefully, stared at the splash, then burst into laughter and jumped until both of them were soaked.
She remembers the slap of tiny boots, the bright yellow raincoat against a grey sky, and the milky scent of her child. For a moment, worries about damp rooms and endless laundry receded.
Rain once only made her anxious. Now a light shower can mean putting on boots and going outside together—or staying in to watch drops and listen. Rain has become part of how mother and daughter discover the world.`},
  'rv-040':{name:'Chloe',role:'University student abroad',city:'Los Angeles',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Life abroad','Homesickness'],title:'Rain That Did Not Smell Like Home',hook:'A rainy day in Los Angeles did not smell like Shenzhen, and homesickness arrived all at once.',body:`During her first winter in Los Angeles, Chloe ran excitedly into the rain and discovered that the wet ground smelled nothing like Shenzhen. Because the rain smelled wrong, she suddenly missed home.
Shenzhen rain in her memory is warm and enveloping; rain abroad feels colder. She no longer deals with mouldy clothes or water beading on the walls, yet part of life seems absent.
She especially remembers her mother complaining about humid weather while cooking red-bean and barley water. The dampness she once disliked has become tied to family and growing up. The smell of one storm made that quiet connection clear.`},
  'rv-039':{name:'Haoran',role:'Recent high-school graduate',city:'Foshan',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['College entrance exam','Family'],title:'His Father Waiting in the Water',hook:'Outside the exam gate, his father stood in floodwater with his trousers rolled up, waving.',body:`Heavy rain fell during the national entrance exam. When Haoran left the school gate, he saw a field of umbrellas and his father standing in water, wearing slippers with his trousers rolled up. Receiving that umbrella felt like the moment high school finally ended.
The fan in the exam room, the ink on the paper, a half-dry uniform, and rain striking concrete all became part of the memory.
Rain had meant wet shoes and cancelled sports classes, but its sound also calmed him during anxious nights of study. The umbrella his father handed him joined the long months of preparation to the relief of walking out.`},
  'rv-002':{name:'Jing',role:'Florist',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Flower shop','Loss'],title:'The Roses Lifted at Midnight',hook:'When muddy water entered the shop, she and her husband lifted bucket after bucket of roses onto higher ground.',body:`On a typhoon night, muddy water spread through Jing’s flower shop. She and her husband stood barefoot in it, lifting buckets of roses onto raised surfaces. Even after the storm, the smell of dirty water remained for a week.
She remembers the lights going out, the blue-grey curtain of rain, and rotting leaves mixed with the scent of flowers. The normally vivid bouquets looked unusually fragile.
Now she checks the hygrometer and petals and starts the dehumidifier whenever rain begins. Humidity affects flowering time, waste, and electricity costs—but gloomy days also bring customers seeking bright flowers for comfort.`},
  'rv-005':{name:'Granny Lin',role:'Retired factory worker',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Old Xiguan','Ageing and the body'],title:'A Childhood Beneath the Arcades',hook:'Outside the eaves rain fell like a waterfall; underneath, she could still run out for sweet soup.',body:`As a child, Granny Lin ran beneath the old arcades with friends even in heavy rain, stopping to buy sweet soup. Water poured beyond the eaves like a waterfall while the covered walkway stayed dry. Rain hammering a metal awning still sounds comforting to her.
Today rain changes her pace. Wet market floors feel dangerous without a walking stick, and long rainy spells make her joints ache and disturb her sleep.
Neighbours who cannot go out gather under a pavilion to play mahjong and talk. What she misses is not only old architecture, but a life in which people shared shelter, looked after one another, and waited together for rain to pass.`},
  'rv-001':{name:'A-Qiang',role:'Food-delivery rider',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Delivery','Income and safety'],title:'The Smashed Birthday Cake',hook:'After the cake was destroyed, he sat in the floodwater for several seconds before getting up.',body:`A-Qiang spends more than ten hours a day on an electric scooter. During Dragon Boat Rain, his tyre crossed wet road paint in Zhujiang New Town and the bike slid out from under him. The birthday cake was ruined; he sat in the water for several seconds before standing.
Rain strikes his helmet visor while sweat builds beneath his raincoat and cold water runs down his neck. On another delivery, a wet phone screen began registering phantom touches and he could not confirm the order.
Rain bonuses can mean more income, but also worse visibility and longer braking distances. As a child he jumped in muddy puddles; now dark clouds make him wonder whether the order will be late—and whether he will fall.`},
  'rv-021':{name:'Lin Yu',role:'Independent bookseller',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Bookshop','Pausing'],title:'A Book Opened While Sheltering',hook:'Rain drove passers-by into the bookshop; when it stopped, some bought the books they had opened.',body:`One weekday afternoon, a sudden downpour sent more than a dozen passers-by into Lin Yu’s shop. Some browsed while standing; others sat quietly on the steps. When the rain stopped, several left with the books they had opened while sheltering.
Lin Yu remembers the dense, muted sound on the patterned glass and the warm air from the dehumidifier. It was a different rainy-day experience from an empty shop.
Rain still means closing windows, moving books, and watching the water tank. Yet readers who linger make the weather memorable. Protecting paper and welcoming people out of the rain happen at the same time.`},
  'rv-038':{name:'Uncle Hua',role:'Lychee farmer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Orchard','Harvest'],title:'The Split Lychee Was Still Sweet',hook:'He tasted a split lychee—still sweet—and began to cry.',body:`Just before harvest, days of heavy rain brought ripe lychees to the ground and split fruit still hanging on the trees. Uncle Hua sat in the mud, tasted one, and found it sweet. Then he cried for the investment and harvest that had been lost.
The orchard smelled faintly fermented, red mud wrapped around his boots, and tool handles swelled in the rain. He often checks drainage channels with a torch and hoe.
Fruit trees need water, but too much can destroy a season. As he grows older, storms make his limits feel sharper. He hopes people eating lychees also remember the days farmers spend watching the sky and clearing drains.`},
  'rv-041':{name:'Xuanxuan',role:'Primary-school student',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Childhood','Adults and children'],title:'Three Snails in a Water Bottle',hook:'He wanted to keep the snails he found after the rain, but his grandmother threw away the bottle.',body:`After a storm, Xuanxuan and his classmates found three large snails in a flower bed and hid them in a water bottle. His grandmother worried about germs, threw the bottle away, and made him wash his hands again and again. He cried for a long time.
He remembers wet soil, slippery shells, and the plastic smell inside his raincoat. He prefers superhero rain boots because an umbrella occupies one of his hands.
Cancelled sports class is boring; jumping in puddles after school is wonderful. Adults worry about dirty shoes, while he wants to see the creatures that emerge after rain. The weather places those two kinds of attention side by side.`},
  'rv-027':{name:'Fang',role:'Convenience-store owner',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Convenience store','Hot food'],title:'The Convenience Store Lit at Midnight',hook:'When strangers came in from the storm, she cooked radish and fish balls for everyone.',body:`A midnight storm flooded the pavement and sent several strangers—drivers and students—into Fang’s convenience store. Everyone was soaked, so she turned the hot-food pot up and cooked radish and fish balls for them.
Outside, wind and rain shook the street. Inside, people talked around the steaming food while the door chime mixed with dripping umbrellas and the smell of broth.
Rain makes floors harder to clean, destroys cardboard quickly, and brings a rush of delivery orders. Fang has come to see the store light as more than illumination for shelves: it offers passers-by somewhere to stop and wait.`},
  'rv-023':{name:'A-Wen',role:'Photographer',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Sounds & Landscapes',tags:['Photography','Improvisation'],title:'Lying Down Beside a Puddle',hook:'Rain ruined the planned shoot, so he lay on the wet road and made his favourite series.',body:`A coastal photo session was interrupted by a thunderstorm. The client’s hair and makeup were wet and everyone was upset. A-Wen asked them to stand with transparent umbrellas beside a puddle, then lay on the road to photograph their reflections. The series became one of his favourites.
His lens kept fogging, the camera body felt cold and slippery, and water ran down his back. Pressing the shutter still felt exhilarating.
Rain can destroy a plan and humidity complicates equipment care, so he watches his dry cabinet closely. Yet he has grown to love reflective, emptied streets after storms. Protecting the lens and finding a new image now happen together.`},
  'rv-006':{name:'Lao Li',role:'Steel fixer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Construction','Work stoppage'],title:'The Site Cabin After the Stop-Work Whistle',hook:'Work stopped, the clothes were still damp, and the day’s wages stopped too.',body:`When the foreman blows the stop-work whistle, Lao Li runs to the site cabin to collect the laundry, even if it is only half dry. Paid by the day, a full day of rain also means a full day without wages.
During one storm, a co-worker nearly fell from wet scaffolding before his safety line caught him. Afterwards no one spoke for a long time. The smell of rust, yellow mud around their shoes, and rain roaring on the cabin roof stayed with him.
Sometimes a stoppage means cards and drinks with the others. Back home in Henan, rain meant hot flatbread. On a Guangzhou worksite, it makes him think of school fees and when work can begin again.`},
  'rv-035':{name:'Uncle Liang',role:'Retired conservation engineer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Sounds & Landscapes',tags:['Historic buildings','Retirement'],title:'A Rattan Chair Beside the Courtyard',hook:'He once inspected whether rain had entered; now he sits by the courtyard and watches it fall.',body:`While restoring the Chen Clan Ancestral Hall, Uncle Liang watched rain gather along the roof ridge and follow the building’s drainage paths. Back then, rain tested the quality of his work: where did the water go, and where did it collect?
Now retired, he still walks to the courtyard when it rains. Grey bricks darken and drops fall from the eaves into a stone jar with a clear, ringing sound.
Decades of work turned watching water into a habit. He once concentrated on keeping it out; now he also values guiding it away. During the rainy season, he sets out a rattan chair, brews Liubao tea, and watches slowly.`},
  'rv-004':{name:'Jiaxin',role:'Physics student',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Campus','Shared life'],title:'Carrying Her Shoes Back to the Dorm',hook:'With no delivery riders taking orders, she and her roommates carried their shoes and waded home.',body:`While revising for first-year exams, Jiaxin and her roommates were trapped in the library by a thunderstorm. No delivery rider accepted their food order, so they took off their shoes and laughed their way through ankle-deep water back to the dorm.
The library air-conditioning contrasted sharply with the humid heat outside. The water around their feet was warm and slightly slippery.
Rain sometimes gives her permission to rest indoors, but days of humidity leave laundry hanging endlessly on the balcony. She also remembers relatives in Chaozhou brewing tea through any storm—the sound of hot water and rain creating another kind of calm.`},
  'rv-012':{name:'Mr. Wang',role:'Ride-hailing driver',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Transport','Inside the car'],title:'The Seat That Never Dried',hook:'Water left by one passenger soaked the dress of the next.',body:`On a rainy airport run, a passenger brought a dripping umbrella into the car and soaked the fabric seat. The next passenger sat down, wet her dress, and an argument and poor rating followed.
Mr. Wang remembers the smell of damp floor mats and streetlights scattering across wet glass. Driving demands more concentration, and after work he still has to remove the mats and draw water from the seat.
Rain can bring more orders, but slippery roads and floodwater also increase risk and cost. He avoids flooded underpasses even when detours upset passengers. The weather affects both how much he earns and how he protects the car that earns it.`},
  'rv-016':{name:'Auntie Huang',role:'Herbal-tea shop owner',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Herbal-tea shop','Neighbourhood'],title:'Ginger Syrup Before the Rain Stopped',hook:'A soaked young woman stood beneath the eaves, and Auntie Huang handed her a warm bowl without charging.',body:`On a grey rainy day, a young woman arrived drenched from work and sheltered beneath Auntie Huang’s awning. Seeing her shiver with tears in her eyes, Huang offered a bowl of hot ginger syrup for free. The woman drank, cried, thanked her, and left when the rain eased.
The medicinal brew bubbled while steam clouded the glass, making the rain outside seem even colder. Rainy seasons are busy, but storing herbs also becomes harder and damp stock must be discarded.
She continues the food traditions of her family and neighbourhood and misses the days when people sat on wooden stools with ceramic bowls, talking as they waited. The drink extended that habit of hospitality to a stranger.`},
  'rv-033':{name:'Lin Sen',role:'Botany researcher',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Sounds & Landscapes',tags:['Plants','Small lives'],title:'Moss Glowing on Dead Wood',hook:'After days of rain, an overlooked log turned bright with translucent green moss.',body:`One morning after prolonged rain, Lin Sen entered the greenhouse and found a dead log covered in translucent green moss. Sunlight passed through the glass and caught droplets on the leaves until the log seemed to glow.
Green is the strongest colour in the memory, followed by the smell of wet soil. He studies ferns and mosses and goes out in rain boots with a camera and magnifier to observe changes normally missed.
Before studying botany, rain simply meant less sunlight. Now it also reveals plants unfurling. He hopes city residents can look beyond wet shoes and notice the small lives among soil, leaves, and branches after rain.`},
  'rv-044':{name:'Siqi',role:'University student seeking work',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Job search','Composure'],title:'High Heels Changed in a Restroom',hook:'Before the interview, she changed into heels while water still dripped from her trousers.',body:`On the way to a final interview in Zhujiang New Town, Siqi was soaked by wind and rain. She hid in an office restroom to change shoes. Her suit trousers clung to her calves, her fringe had collapsed, and she felt painfully dishevelled as she entered the room.
Wet fabric and cold air-conditioning made her shiver, and her soles sounded too loud on marble. She had packed the heels and worn old trainers outside, yet still could not keep dry.
Rain can damage a résumé, delay a train, and threaten punctuality. In her first year of university it meant staying in the dorm; during a job search, it became one more threshold to working life.`},
  'rv-007':{name:'Tao',role:'Appliance-store manager',city:'Foshan',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Home appliances','Business'],title:'Sales Collected in a Water Tank',hook:'While customers worried about wet walls, he moved the dehumidifiers to the entrance.',body:`During long wet spells, Tao moves dehumidifiers and clothes dryers to the most visible part of the shop and runs demonstrations. On one humid day the entire stock sold out, and two customers even competed for the dusty display model.
He likes pulling out a machine’s heavy water tank: it is proof of performance and a measure of sales. On busy days he repeats the same explanation until his voice turns hoarse.
Yet the person selling dryers may still arrive in damp clothes. Rain once cancelled his basketball games; now it brings peak season and commission. His own humid life and the business of solving humidity happen on the same day.`},
  'rv-024':{name:'Auntie Zhong',role:'Stray-cat rescue volunteer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Stray animals','Concern'],title:'A Faint Cry Inside the Pipe',hook:'Rain almost drowned out the kittens. She aimed a torch into a pipe filling with water.',body:`On a stormy night, Auntie Zhong heard faint cries near a residential ventilation opening. By torchlight she found a mother cat and newborn kittens inside a pipe about to flood. Kneeling in muddy water, she lifted the kittens out one by one.
The rain was deafening; the kittens felt cold and trembled in her hands, while the smell of the drain settled into her clothes. Since beginning rescue work, rain on her window also makes her think of shelters outside.
Feeding must be timed between showers, and every shelter needs checking for leaks. Rain once meant inconvenience for shopping and shoes; now it means concern for lives beyond the eaves.`},
  'rv-043':{name:'Xiaowen',role:'Art-exam student',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Art exam','Paper'],title:'A Hole in the Drawing Paper',hook:'The eraser barely touched the highlight, but the damp paper tore. She ripped it up and began again.',body:`During days of rain before an art exam, Xiaowen’s drawing paper absorbed moisture and softened. When an eraser passed over a highlight, a hole opened in the surface. Weeks of pressure broke through and she cried as she tore up the portrait and started again.
The studio smelled of graphite, paint, and turpentine; the paper felt cold and spongy. With gouache, she often paints with one hand and holds a hairdryer in the other.
She now seals paper in moisture-proof bags and checks windows and paint boxes. Rain can create beautiful soft edges, but during exam preparation it mostly affects her work, assignments, and how much she can finish in a day.`},
  'rv-022':{name:'Captain Liang',role:'Pearl River cruise captain',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Pearl River','Navigation'],title:'Tianzi Wharf Behind a Curtain of Rain',hook:'He eased the boat back to the wharf and only noticed his soaked back after every passenger was ashore.',body:`Near Canton Tower, a violent storm suddenly pressed across the river. Vapour obscured the view and thunder sounded close. Captain Liang used radar and years of experience to bring the boat slowly back to Tianzi Wharf. Only after every passenger left did he notice that his back was wet.
The hull rolled, river water mixed with diesel in the air, and neon lights dissolved into halos. Above all, he remembers responsibility for everyone aboard.
Descended from a water-dwelling family, he grew up hearing elders teach respect for water. Rain and river level shape his schedule, and safety always comes before the beauty of a misty river.`},
  'rv-010':{name:'Zhao Yang',role:'Social-media manager',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Moving city','Humid spring weather'],title:'The Leather Jacket That Grew Mould',hook:'Soon after moving to Shenzhen, his favourite jacket acquired a layer of white-green fuzz.',body:`Not long after moving from Beijing, Zhao Yang encountered Shenzhen’s humid spring weather. Glass filled with droplets, the living-room wall seemed to weep, crisps went soft, and white-green mould appeared on his favourite leather jacket.
The experience rewrote his assumptions. Clothes that dried in a day in the north remained wet for days. He began checking moisture absorbers, closing windows, and treating a dehumidifier as essential.
At first the coolness of rain felt new; later sticky sheets and damp clothing made him want to return north. A jacket, a few crisps, and daily laundry quietly revised his romantic idea of southern life.`},
  'rv-025':{name:'Coach Chen',role:'PE teacher and football coach',city:'Dongguan',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['School','Football'],title:'A Handshake in the Rain After Defeat',hook:'They lost, then lined up to shake hands in the rain. He still remembers the light in their eyes.',body:`Heavy rain began during the second half of a school final. Coach Chen watched the children slip on wet grass, get up, and keep chasing the ball. They lost, but lined up to shake hands in the rain, their eyes still bright.
The match left memories of rubber, waterlogged shoes, and shouting through rain. It was more vivid than his usual work of stopping play and moving lessons indoors.
Long wet spells disrupt teaching and increase safety checks and conversations with parents. Yet he also remembers kicking pomelos through muddy fields as a child in Meizhou. Rain holds worry, movement, friendship, and youth at once.`},
  'rv-008':{name:'Juan',role:'Seafood-stall owner',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Wet market','Livelihood'],title:'Catching Their Own Fish in the Aisle',hook:'The market flooded and the aerators stopped, so every seller chased fish through the aisle.',body:`During Dragon Boat Rain, water entered the market and a power cut stopped the tank aerators. Loaches, prawns, and snakehead fish escaped into the aisle. Stallholders chased their own stock with nets, cursing and laughing at the absurdity.
Juan remembers the intensified smell of seafood and drains beneath the roof, and rubber boots slapping through water. Rain reduces customers, delays delivery trucks, and leaves perishable stock with nowhere to wait.
Growing up in Zhanjiang, she thought coastal storms were magnificent. From a four-square-metre stall, she first lays down cardboard against slipping and watches delivery messages. Rain immediately touches the day’s income.`},
  'rv-017':{name:'Lin Ming',role:'Archivist',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Documents','Preserving memory'],title:'When the Repository Dehumidifiers Stopped',hook:'After the typhoon cut power, the familiar hum vanished while the hygrometer kept rising.',body:`During Typhoon Mangkhut in 2018, a fault in the main and backup power stopped the repository’s dehumidification system. Lin Ming and colleagues worked by torchlight, pushing dry boxes through the hot stacks to rescue Republican-era newspapers.
Machines that normally ran continuously fell silent, making the smell of old paper and camphorwood stronger. The hardest part was watching the humidity climb without being able to restore control.
Lin Ming normally monitors temperature and humidity and checks seals and storage. After moving from Beijing, moisture became part of archival work. Rain can affect not only clothes, but the history held on paper.`},
  'rv-042':{name:'A-Jie',role:'Boarding-school student',city:'Foshan',theme:'Growing Up & Belonging',tags:['Boarding school','Independent living'],title:'Six Students Sharing One Hairdryer',hook:'After the dorm flooded, they took turns drying the uniforms needed the next morning.',body:`In his first year of boarding school, heavy rain and humid weather blocked the balcony drain and flooded the room. A-Jie and five roommates bailed water with washbasins. Clothes stayed wet for a week, so they shared one hairdryer until it began to smoke.
He remembers sour laundry, trainers, and the sticky feeling of running in damp socks. Thunder during class immediately makes him think of clothes left outside.
At home his parents handled transport and washing. Boarding taught him to pack spare socks and invent ways to dry things. Rain might cancel morning exercise and offer extra sleep, but it also turns independence into a series of practical tasks.`},
  'rv-030':{name:'A-Jian',role:'Traffic police motorcycle officer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Traffic','Rescue'],title:'A Driver Pulled from the Underpass',hook:'He and a colleague entered waist-deep water and brought a trapped driver to safety.',body:`During a storm, water rose rapidly in an underpass and stalled a car. A-Jian and a colleague waded into waist-deep muddy water, broke a window, and led the driver to safety. Only afterwards did he notice that sheet metal underwater had cut his foot.
Rain drowned out the radio, so they had to shout. Police lights reflected across the water while the ground beneath it remained invisible.
Wet-weather patrols include closing roads, pushing cars, and clearing branches. By the time he removes his raincoat, sweat has soaked the uniform underneath. Rain once meant listening from indoors; in uniform, it means reaching the junction.`},
  'rv-031':{name:'Susu',role:'Arcade café owner',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Sounds & Landscapes',tags:['Arcades','Pausing'],title:'Two People Drawing Closer Under the Arcade',hook:'In the downpour they stood close beneath the arcade, tilting the umbrella slightly to one side.',body:`One evening, a young couple ran beneath the arcade outside Susu’s café. The man tilted the umbrella towards the woman and they stood close together, silently watching the rain. Susu remembered the wordless moment.
On rainy days she turns off several ceiling lights, leaves warm lamps on, and chooses slower music. Coffee mixes with the earthy smell of wet stone as light catches water falling from the eaves.
Rain is not always good for business, but those who stay often order a hot drink, read, or simply pause. Susu sees both the shelter offered by the arcade and the way people slow down within it.`},
  'rv-013':{name:'Xiao Chen',role:'Pest-control technician',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Pest control','Housing'],title:'The Soaked Beam in the Attic',hook:'His torch revealed a hollowed wooden beam while the homeowner trembled below.',body:`Treating termites in an old Xiguan house, Xiao Chen climbed into the attic and found a load-bearing beam hollowed from within. Winged insects clung to the wall while the elderly homeowner stood trembling below.
His protective suit felt stifling, his goggles fogged, and rotten wood mixed with the smell of chemicals. In the rainy season he carries tanks up old stairways and finishes each visit soaked through.
His elders once used lamps and bowls of water against flying insects. Now rain makes him think not only of streets, but of life hidden in trunks, drains, and wall cracks. Humidity brings those unseen spaces into daily life.`},
  'rv-037':{name:'Vivian',role:'Strategy consultant',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Flights','Disrupted plans'],title:'Six Hours Waiting in the Cabin',hook:'The third round of biscuits was gone, and the flight still had no departure time.',body:`Travelling to Beijing to sign a contract, Vivian spent six hours inside a grounded aircraft during thunderstorms. Cabin crew distributed biscuits three times while announcements repeated that there was no departure time. She lost the opportunity when she missed the signing and cried in her seat.
She remembers the cramped cabin, the stale smell of airline meals, diagonal rain on the window, and a chorus of sighs and call bells.
Frequent travel has taught her to check flight status and search for backup trains whenever rain appears. Yet even the best-prepared plan can become impossible to control while waiting inside an aircraft.`},
  'rv-011':{name:'Qiang',role:'Car-wash owner',city:'Foshan',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Car washing','Waiting'],title:'A Downpour on the Car Just Washed',hook:'The polished car had barely left the ramp when the rain began.',body:`Several employees spent nearly an hour polishing a black Mercedes. As soon as the owner drove up the ramp, a downpour began. The car returned and its owner demanded a refund or another wash. Qiang remembers his exhausted workers staring at the rain in frustration.
During long wet spells, customers disappear while rent and wages remain. He sits at the entrance with his staff, looking idle while worrying constantly.
When the weather clears, mud-streaked cars form a queue and everyone works from morning to night. Forecasts have become part of the business: rainy days mean waiting, clear days mean rushing, and neither allows much rest.`},
  'rv-019':{name:'Mr. Chen',role:'Slipper-factory owner',city:'Jieyang',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Manufacturing','Footwear'],title:'Slippers Made for the Rain',hook:'As soon as widespread rain is forecast, the factory begins overtime production.',body:`When widespread rain is forecast across Guangdong, Mr. Chen schedules overtime and contacts wholesalers. He remembers a late-1990s storm when the factory shipped plastic slippers overnight to Tianhe and sold one hundred thousand pairs in three days.
The workshop smells of heated plastic, and the rhythm of sole presses mixes with rain outside. This weather brings orders, but also complicates delivery.
Rain moved slippers from indoor footwear into commuting and everyday streets. Customers once prioritised low price and water resistance; now they also ask about grip and comfort. What people wear in rain continually reshapes what the factory makes.`},
  'rv-014':{name:'Xiaotong',role:'Kindergarten teacher',city:'Zhuhai',theme:'Shelter & Care',tags:['Kindergarten','Caring for children'],title:'Thirty Pairs of Little Rain Boots',hook:'Rain began at pickup time: parents crowded beneath the canopy while children cried inside.',body:`A downpour began exactly at dismissal. Parents crowded under the kindergarten’s narrow canopy while children waited and cried. One boy ran into the water in his favourite boots, slipped into mud, and his frightened mother lost her temper. Xiaotong remembers the chaos vividly.
Rain hammered the metal roof above the children’s shouts. During wet spells she changes outdoor lessons, watches slippery floors, and helps dry socks and shoes.
She also brings children to the window to listen, draw umbrellas, and read picture books. Rain increases the burden of care and safety, but it can also become a lesson in observing nature.`},
  'rv-026':{name:'Lao Guo',role:'Exterior waterproofing worker',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Leak repair','Safety'],title:'Black Clouds Outside the Twentieth Floor',hook:'Suspended twenty floors up, wind drove him against the glass until someone inside caught the rope.',body:`While waterproofing an office tower, black clouds suddenly brought violent rain. Lao Guo hung outside the twentieth floor as wind pushed him against the curtain wall. He gripped a window frame, rain filling his eyes, until someone inside opened a vent and caught the rope.
Rain makes him think first about safety. At the first drops, exterior workers need to return to the ground and appointments must be changed.
Work stoppages mean lost income, but storms also reveal cracks and bring a wave of repair calls. Years in the trade have trained him to watch how water runs and wind drives it into walls. Rain interrupts the work and identifies where work is needed.`},
  'rv-045':{name:'Zheng',role:'Agricultural ecology doctoral student',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Agricultural research','Field plots'],title:'The Experimental Field Washed Flat',hook:'At two in the morning, he and his supervisor stood in muddy water rescuing equipment.',body:`In the second year of his doctorate, a midnight storm flooded the farm. Zheng and his supervisor waded through mud with torches, rescuing equipment while plots prepared over months were washed flat. He cried, afraid that both his research and graduation would be delayed.
Soil and fertiliser filled the air, water rose above his boots, and each step stuck in the mud. Torchlight revealed only dense lines of rain.
Forecasts now make him think first of sensors and soil samples. Heavy rain can destroy an experiment, but it may also produce unexpected data. Agricultural ecology has taught him that research ultimately returns to fields—and to weather.`},
  'rv-029':{name:'Dr. Xu',role:'Traditional Chinese medicine practitioner',city:'Foshan',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Traditional clinic','Local customs'],title:'Rainy-Day Habits in the Clinic',hook:'Rain sounded outside while herbs and moxa were prepared within.',body:`During long wet spells, Dr. Xu asks apprentices to prepare herbs and count moxa sticks and cupping jars. He remembers a young newcomer to Foshan who arrived describing fatigue and bodily heaviness. Such rainy-day encounters are part of how he observes local life.
The clinic mixes the smell of wet soil with moxa smoke, while rain continues outside. Humidity also complicates herb storage and increases waste.
After decades of practice, Dr. Xu uses traditional Chinese medicine’s language of dampness to interpret climate and the body. Family soups and street-side herbal tea belong to the same familiar world. This story records one practitioner’s way of understanding the rainy season.`},
  'rv-018':{name:'Xiao Zhang',role:'Estate agent',city:'Shenzhen',theme:'Living with Humidity',tags:['Housing','Property viewing'],title:'Opening a Ground-Floor Door in the Rain',hook:'The clients touched the corner of the wall and immediately walked away.',body:`Early in his career, Xiao Zhang showed a ground-floor flat during humid spring weather. The floor was wet and droplets covered the wallpaper. A client touched a mouldy corner, looked at their hand, and left.
He remembers the earthy smell of the empty flat and slippery tiles. Now he turns on lights and air-conditioning before a viewing, knowing that dampness shapes both perception and sale.
Some buyers deliberately inspect homes after heavy rain to find stains around walls and windows. Rain may obstruct a sale, but it also exposes problems that future residents need to see.`},
  'rv-015':{name:'A-Fei',role:'Campsite operator',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Camping','Weather and business'],title:'Waking the Tents at Two in the Morning',hook:'He opened every tent and moved sleeping guests into the brick reception building.',body:`A holiday storm overwhelmed the campsite drainage after midnight and water rose above the ankles. With a torch, A-Fei opened each tent, woke the guests, and moved everyone into the brick reception centre. By morning, expensive tents lay in yellow mud.
Rain on the canopy was so loud that people had to shout. Grass became mud that held onto boots. Even after rain stopped, the site could not reopen until the ground and equipment dried.
As a camper, he once treated rain as adventure. As an operator, it means cancellations, damage, and recovery. Some guests still come to brew coffee and listen, but leisure and responsibility feel very different beneath the same canopy.`},
  'rv-036':{name:'Engineer Chen',role:'Weather forecaster',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Weather','Night shift'],title:'The Radar Screen at Three in the Morning',hook:'Before the city woke, Engineer Chen was already watching deep-red shapes on the radar.',body:`One memorable shift began at three in the morning. Intense rainfall echoes remained over Guangzhou, and Engineer Chen helped assess conditions and issue a rainstorm warning. Looking out at the morning rain, it was impossible not to think about travel and work across the city.
The forecasting room was cold with air-conditioning and machines hummed continuously. Outside rain became changing numbers and colours on a screen.
Rainy seasons bring consecutive night shifts, days away from home, and the uncertainty of prediction. He hopes that people receiving a warning message also remember those who watched the screen through the night.`},
  'rv-020':{name:'Technician Chen',role:'Air-conditioner repairer',city:'Guangzhou',theme:'Working in the Rain',tags:['Repair','Work at height'],title:'The Drainpipe Outside the Window',hook:'While the customer waited indoors, he searched for a blockage on the fifteenth-floor wall.',body:`A customer’s air-conditioner kept leaking, so Technician Chen climbed onto the exterior of the fifteenth floor and found a blocked drainpipe. When he cleared it, foul standing water sprayed across his face. He remembers the slippery bracket and cold rain running down his neck.
Before a rainy callout, he checks his safety rope and shoe soles. On busy days he visits several homes and remains wet from either sweat or rain.
More repairs can mean more income, but height, slippery surfaces, and electricity also raise the danger. Rain now makes him ask whether he can work safely—and whether people indoors recognise the labour behind dry air.`}
};

const PLUM_RAIN_CARDS={
  '广州':{category:'Architecture',subject:'Xiguan Qilou Arcades',title:'Eaves Joined into a Street',body:'Guangzhou’s qilou arcades extend upper rooms above the pavement, creating a continuous sheltered colonnade. Rain may cover the street while people still pass shops, stop, and talk beneath the roof. The building leaves a whole street able to keep walking and trading.',imageAlt:'Xiguan qilou arcade'},
  '深圳':{category:'Urban Infrastructure',subject:'Sponge Features in Xiangmi Park',title:'Leaving Room for Rainwater',body:'Bioswales, sunken gardens, and a lake in Xiangmi Park help collect and hold rain. Water passes through plants and soil; some soaks into the ground and some is stored for reuse. An ordinary patch of parkland can also be a place where the city receives a storm.',imageAlt:'Sponge-city features in Xiangmi Park'},
  '珠海':{category:'Food',subject:'Tangjiawan Luodou Rice Dumpling',title:'Rainy Season Wrapped in Leaves',body:'Tangjiawan luodou rice dumplings wrap glutinous rice and fillings in locally common pandan-like leaves. Their long shape carries a fresh leaf fragrance. The Dragon Boat Festival also falls near Guangdong’s concentrated rains, joining leaf, steam, and rainfall in a seasonal Zhuhai scene.',imageAlt:'Tangjiawan luodou rice dumpling'},
  '汕头':{category:'Architecture',subject:'Small Park Arcade District',title:'Walking Through Rain Beneath the Colonnade',body:'Shantou’s Small Park streets radiate from the Sun Yat-sen Memorial Pavilion. Arcades connect shelter from sun and rain along the shops. When the road is wet, the colonnade remains a meeting place for pedestrians and businesses—and a route through the old city’s commercial memory.',imageAlt:'Small Park arcade district in Shantou'},
  '佛山':{category:'Festival Objects',subject:'Jiujiang Dragon Boat and Paddles',title:'Dragon-Boat Paddles in the Rainy Season',body:'Jiujiang’s dragon-boat culture lives in boats, wooden paddles, drums, and coordinated movement on water. Festival races often meet Guangdong’s Dragon Boat Rain. A paddle entering the river joins rainy-season water, bodily rhythm, and a celebration completed together.',imageAlt:'Jiujiang dragon boat and wooden paddles'},
  '韶关':{category:'Architecture',subject:'Changwei Village Enclosed House',title:'Helping Timber Endure Humidity',body:'The enclosed house at Changwei Village combines river stone, grey brick, tile, and timber. Its fir corridors and roof structure carry domestic life. Protecting materials from damp and decay is part of preserving the building; keeping one roof also means caring for what it is made of.',imageAlt:'Enclosed house at Changwei Village'},
  '河源':{category:'Food',subject:'Beitou Rice Noodles of Lianping',title:'Rice Noodles Wait for a Clear Day',body:'Handmade Beitou rice noodles are laid on small bamboo trays to dry, then stored somewhere ventilated and dry. Fine strands move from wet to dry, and their production follows the weather. One serving reveals the sunlight, time, and waiting behind food.',imageAlt:'Beitou rice noodles drying'},
  '梅州':{category:'Architecture',subject:'Pond and Drainage of a Weilong House',title:'A House Gives Water Somewhere to Go',body:'Meizhou’s weilong houses often combine a crescent pond, threshing ground, and curved residential compound. Courtyards, channels, and ponds remain working parts of the home. Following water through the site reveals how Hakka architecture places dwelling and water management in one layout.',imageAlt:'Pond and drainage of a Meizhou weilong house'},
  '惠州':{category:'Food',subject:'Huizhou Preserved Mustard Greens',title:'Keeping the Taste of Fresh Greens',body:'Huizhou meicai begins with mustard greens and passes through salting, drying, and resting. Drying must respond to the weather, and moisture becomes part of the recipe. From fresh plant to preserved food, the local flavour carries careful control of water and storage.',imageAlt:'Huizhou preserved mustard greens'},
  '汕尾':{category:'Harbour Life',subject:'Shanwei Fishing Harbour',title:'Before the Storm, Boats Return',body:'Shanwei’s harbour is where fishing boats leave and return. Before a typhoon, boats come in for shelter and moorings are checked and reinforced. Dense hulls, quays, and reflections show how coastal life follows the weather—and what a safe return means.',imageAlt:'Fishing boats in Shanwei harbour'},
  '东莞':{category:'Everyday Object',subject:'Dongguan Grass Mat',title:'A Mat Grown Beside the Water',body:'Dongguan grass once grew beside waterways and tidal flats before becoming mats, baskets, and household objects. After harvest it must be prepared and dried before weaving. A grass mat carries material knowledge from a wet landscape into the home.',imageAlt:'A woven Dongguan grass mat'},
  '中山':{category:'Food',subject:'Huangpu Cured Meats',title:'Flavour Has Wet and Dry Seasons',body:'Traditional Huangpu cured meats use the drier air of autumn and winter, now often supplemented by controlled drying. Flavour develops as moisture leaves the meat. Rows of sausages show how production follows seasonal humidity and how technology adjusts dependence on weather.',imageAlt:'Huangpu cured meats drying'},
  '江门':{category:'Food',subject:'Xinhui Aged Citrus Peel',title:'Aged Fragrance Needs Care',body:'Peel from Xinhui citrus is opened, sun-dried, stored, and aged into chenpi. Temperature and humidity must be managed, and clear days are used to air the peel again. Its fragrance contains time, repeated inspection, and care—made especially tangible by damp weather.',imageAlt:'Xinhui citrus peel drying'},
  '阳江':{category:'Everyday Object',subject:'Traditional Lacquered Leather Trunk',title:'A Protective Layer Around the Trunk',body:'Historically, Yangjiang lacquered leather trunks travelled overseas with emigrants. Their lacquered surfaces resisted water and humidity, giving everyday belongings another layer of protection. One old case reveals how materials and craft answered moisture during long journeys.',imageAlt:'Traditional lacquered leather trunk'},
  '湛江':{category:'Food',subject:'Zhanjiang Dried Fish',title:'Sea Flavour on the Drying Ground',body:'Zhanjiang dried fish is salted, dried, and conditioned through several stages. Moisture affects texture, so sunlight and time become ingredients. Wet weather makes the conditions for drying visible; preserving a catch is also work negotiated with the sky.',imageAlt:'Fish drying in Zhanjiang'},
  '茂名':{category:'Food',subject:'Lychees',title:'Ripening Still Means Watching the Sky',body:'During the Maoming lychee harvest, growers watch for heavy rain and strong wind. Water nourishes trees, but too much may split fruit and wind can bring it down. Every basket contains judgements about ripeness and weather; the rainy season enters the orchard and the table.',imageAlt:'Lychees being harvested and sorted'},
  '肇庆':{category:'Architecture',subject:'Licha Ancient Village in Gaoyao',title:'Beside Water, Build on Higher Ground',body:'Licha village sits around water with houses arranged on a small rise. Historic floods influenced the choice to live higher, while drainage and embankments helped protect the settlement. Its ponds, lanes, and terrain reveal a community gradually adapting to changing water.',imageAlt:'Licha Ancient Village and surrounding water'},
  '清远':{category:'Everyday Object',subject:'Yingde Black Tea and a Sealed Caddy',title:'Keeping the Fragrance of Dry Tea',body:'Yingde black tea reaches the table after withering, rolling, oxidation, and drying. Finished tea still faces moisture in the air, so packaging and sealed containers protect its flavour. In the rainy season, one small caddy connects local tea to indoor humidity.',imageAlt:'Yingde black tea and a sealed caddy'},
  '潮州':{category:'Everyday Object',subject:'Gongfu Tea Set',title:'A Tea Table Beside the Sound of Rain',body:'Chaozhou gongfu tea joins warming the pot, rinsing cups, serving tea, and welcoming guests. Small vessels make an intimate everyday space. Rain strikes the eaves while hot water flows; a cup of tea leaves time to sit and talk through the season.',imageAlt:'Chaozhou gongfu tea setting'},
  '揭阳':{category:'Everyday Object',subject:'Plastic Slippers',title:'A Pair of Shoes for Rain',body:'Jieyang’s plastic-footwear industry sends light, water-resistant objects into countless homes. When shoes and socks are easily soaked, washable slippers embody a material choice. Beneath an ordinary sole lies a connection between local factories and rainy-day needs.',imageAlt:'Plastic slippers made in Jieyang'},
  '云浮':{category:'Food',subject:'Xinxing Preserved Plums',title:'Turning Fresh Fruit Sweet and Sour',body:'Xinxing preserved plums are salted, desalted, and dried several times until fresh fruit becomes a lasting snack. Drying ties production to clear and rainy days and demands careful moisture control. Each sweet-sour piece keeps local knowledge of fruit, water, and time.',imageAlt:'Xinxing preserved plums drying'}
};

window.PLUM_RAIN_EN={cityNames:PLUM_RAIN_CITY_NAMES,voices:PLUM_RAIN_VOICES,cards:PLUM_RAIN_CARDS};
